WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.462
A cat and a dog, on one stage. Two
objects, and two different plans.

2
00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:14.465
Each object needs its own blocks.
Plan each one before you build it.

3
00:00:14.965 --> 00:00:18.077
A program can have more
than one thing inside it.

4
00:00:18.297 --> 00:00:22.476
A cat, a dog, a ball. Each
one is called an object.

5
00:00:22.696 --> 00:00:26.597
An object is a thing that can
be given its own instructions.

6
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:30.950
The cat's blocks move the cat.
The dog's blocks move the dog.

7
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:36.325
Blocks make a program. A program tells
the computer how to run an algorithm.

8
00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:40.355
Before you build, plan.
What should each object do?

9
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.661
The cat walks to the tree, then
says hello. That is its plan.

10
00:00:44.881 --> 00:00:48.550
The dog jumps twice, then
spins. That is a different plan.

11
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.207
The dog is a different object,
so it gets different blocks.

12
00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:56.467
What should the tree do? Nothing.
It gets no blocks at all.

13
00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:01.239
Now build. Turn each plan into
blocks, one object at a time.

14
00:01:01.459 --> 00:01:06.939
Build the cat's program first:
move right, move right, say hello.

15
00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:11.989
Run it. Did the cat do its plan?
Yes it did. That was a test.

16
00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:18.014
Now build the dog's program: jump,
jump, spin. Run that one too.

17
00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:22.321
Test each program as you build
it. Then every bug stays small.

18
00:01:22.821 --> 00:01:27.000
An object's plan can use the repeat
block, like any other program.

19
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.400
The dog must move left three times.
That is three move left blocks.

20
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.939
Or use repeat 3 times, then move
left. That is only two blocks.

21
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:40.246
Same moves, fewer blocks. The
repeat block always goes first.

22
00:01:40.746 --> 00:01:44.043
A stage can hold several
objects at the same time.

23
00:01:44.263 --> 00:01:48.628
Each one carries its own blocks,
and they all run side by side.

24
00:01:48.848 --> 00:01:52.471
So when something goes wrong,
ask which object is doing it.

25
00:01:52.691 --> 00:01:56.034
Check that object's blocks.
Leave the other objects alone.

26
00:01:56.534 --> 00:02:00.574
Real programs have lots of
objects, each with its own plan.

27
00:02:00.794 --> 00:02:04.603
A game has a player and
enemies. Each one is an object.

28
00:02:04.823 --> 00:02:09.188
An animation has several characters.
They all run at the same time.

29
00:02:09.408 --> 00:02:13.216
A traffic light has three
lights: red, amber and green.

30
00:02:13.436 --> 00:02:17.337
Each light is an object with
its own plan for when to be on.

31
00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:22.017
Every object in a program gets
its own plan and its own blocks.

32
00:02:22.237 --> 00:02:25.673
Plan it, build the program,
then run it and test it.

33
00:02:25.893 --> 00:02:29.655
On one stage, every object
quietly does its own job.
